{"type": "model", "t_ms": 80971, "ts": 1795232851.4964721, "turn": 2, "prompt_tokens": 3118, "completion_tokens": 2695, "finish_reason": "stop", "reasoning_tokens": null, "ttft_ms": 4621, "latency_ms": 78263, "usage_source": "reported", "cache_hit_tokens": null, "cache_miss_tokens": null, "error": "", "text": "{\"thought\": \"Three moduli share primes pairwise. Factor n1 and n2 to find p, then derive q and r. Then decrypt c (which is m^(e^3) mod each n) by taking e-th root three times, or just compute m = c^(d) mod n where d is inverse of e^3 mod phi. Easier: since c = m^(e^3) mod n, compute d = inverse(e^3, phi(n)), m = c^d mod n. Let me factor using GCD of moduli.\", \"action\": \"python\", \"code\": \"from math import gcd\\\\\\\\ne=65537\\\\np=gcd(n1,n2)\\nq=n1//p\\nr=n2//p\\nprint('p',p)\\nprint('q',q)\\nprint('r',r)\\nprint('check n3', q*r==n3)\\nphi=(p-1)*(q-1)\\nfrom pow import *\\n\"}"}
