{"type": "model", "t_ms": 236942, "ts": 1795233007.4675555, "turn": 5, "prompt_tokens": 10308, "completion_tokens": 1953, "finish_reason": "stop", "reasoning_tokens": null, "ttft_ms": 1126, "latency_ms": 55428, "usage_source": "reported", "cache_hit_tokens": null, "cache_miss_tokens": null, "error": "", "text": "{\"thought\": \"Still not the flag. The sequential encryption: c starts as m, then c=pow(c,e,n1), c=pow(c,e,n2), c=pow(c,e,n3). So final c = ((m^e mod n1)^e mod n2)^e mod n3. To reverse, I need to take e-th root mod n3 first, then mod n2, then mod n1. But m^e mod n1 is not necessarily < n2, so the intermediate values are reduced mod different moduli. This is tricky. Actually since m < min(n1,n2,n3), and m^e mod n1 gives a value < n1. Then that value raised to e mod n2... The intermediate value c1 = m^e mod n1. c2 = c1^e mod n2. c3 = c2^e mod n3 = c. To reverse: c2 = c^(d3) mod n3 where d3 = e^-1 mod phi(n3). Then c1 = c2^(d2) mod n2. Then m = c1^(d1) mod n1. Let me do that.\", \"action\": \"python\", \"code\": \"from Crypto.Util.number import long_to_bytes\\\\\\\\ne=65537\\\\nn1=p*q; n2=p*r; n3=q*r\\nphi1=(p-1)*(q-1); phi2=(p-1)*(r-1); phi3=(q-1)*(r-1)\\nd3=pow(e,-1,phi3)\\nc2=pow(c,d3,n3)\\nd2=pow(e,-1,phi2)\\nc1=pow(c2,d2,n2)\\nd1=pow(e,-1,phi1)\\nm=pow(c1,d1,n1)\\nprint(long_to_bytes(m))\"}"}
